---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/nav/SiteHeader.astro';
import TopicList from '../../components/overview/TopicList.astro';
import { CFD_URL, DOCS_URL, GITHUB_URL, QUANTUM_URL } from '../../consts';

const unifiedMathReadme = `${GITHUB_URL}/blob/main/deep_causality_unified_math/README.md`;
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Overview — DeepCausality"
  description="DeepCausality is an open-source Rust library for dynamic causal reasoning: causal discovery from data, a one-directional causal order with any computation inside each step, a context that changes while a model runs, actions fired from causal results, and norms that check them."
>
  <SiteHeader />
  <article class="static-page">
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">Overview</p>
      <h1>Cause and effect in systems that change dynamically</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        DeepCausality, an open-source Rust library for dynamic causal reasoning, runs a model as a
        chain of causal steps whose state, environment and rules may change while it runs. At any
        step you can ask what would have happened under other conditions, and an audit log records
        how each answer came about.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>The idea: two layers of composition</h2>
      <p>
        The classical formulation of causality, "if A then B, and if not A then not B," ties the
        direction of cause to the direction of time, so it cannot express the time-symmetric
        fundamental equations of physics.
      </p>
      <p>
        DeepCausality separates the two. The outer layer fixes the causal order: steps compose by
        monadic bind, so each step runs after the one before it and receives its result. The inner
        layer, the step itself, holds an ordinary Rust function that can compute anything, including
        a time-reversible law of physics. A satellite propagated forward and then backward in time
        forms a two-step chain that runs forward in causal order.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each step receives three channels from the step before it: the <em>effect</em>, its result;
        the <em>state</em>, memory carried forward; and the <em>context</em>, the environment. An
        append-only log records what each step did. The <a href="/overview/dynamic-causality/">dynamic causality</a> page shows both layers in code.
      </p>

      <h2>The project at a glance</h2>
      <p>
        The causal monad sequences a model: it decides which step runs after which and passes state
        and context from one step to the next. Causaloids give a model its structure: a single cause,
        a collection of causes whose results combine, or a graph of causes that branch and rejoin.
        The two compose: a causaloid can serve as one step of a monadic chain, and a chain can run
        inside a causaloid, so each part of a model takes the form that suits it. The <a href="/examples/aerospace-flight-envelope/">flight envelope monitor</a> runs a collection of sensor checks, a chain of state-estimation steps and a graph of
        envelope protections in one process.
      </p>
      <p>
        A model often starts from data: causal discovery measures how information about a target
        splits among its sources and ranks the likely causes of a failure. A context holds the world
        the steps read: sensor data, clocks and positions. You can update it between evaluations,
        swap in a counterfactual world, or keep it in a store. The Causal State Machine fires an
        action when a causal result is active, and the Effect Ethos checks a proposed action against
        norms you encode, so a system can refuse an action before it runs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each part ships as its own crate, so a project takes only what it needs: a chain of physics
        steps runs on the causal monad alone, a monitoring system adds a context, the state machine
        and the Effect Ethos, and an incident investigation may need only causal discovery. The five
        topics below cover each part in the order a model is built.
      </p>

      <TopicList label="Five topics" />

      <h2>One mathematics stack underneath</h2>
      <p>
        Seventeen crates supply the mathematics: numeric traits, abstract and linear algebra,
        statistics, FFT, calculus with automatic differentiation, tensors, geometric algebra,
        topology with discrete exterior calculus, and uncertain values. They share one set of
        categorical traits, so a tensor can hold multivectors and one <code>fmap</code> transforms
        every cell. None of them requires an external dependency.
      </p>
      <p>
        One type alias sets a whole program's precision. Switch it between <code>BFloat16</code>, <code>f32</code>, <code>f64</code> and <code>Float106</code>, a
        double-double type with about 31 decimal digits, and every crate follows. The <a href={unifiedMathReadme}>unified math README</a> covers the crates, the shared traits and
        the precision rules; the <a href="/examples/mathematics/">mathematics examples</a> show them
        at work.
      </p>

      <h2>Physics, fluids and quantum models</h2>
      <p>
        Three domain crates build on the causal core. <code>deep_causality_physics</code> supplies
        physics kernels, many wrapped as single causal steps. <code>deep_causality_cfd</code> holds computational fluid dynamics solvers and the Flow DSL. <code>deep_causality_quantum</code> models quantum causal systems with density matrices,
        channels and gates. The fluid and quantum work have their own sites at <a href={CFD_URL}>cfd.deepcausality.com</a> and <a href={QUANTUM_URL}>quantum.deepcausality.com</a>; <a href="/projects/">Projects</a> lists
        both.
      </p>

      <h2>Built for assurance</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No unsafe code.</strong> A workspace lint sets <code>unsafe_code = "forbid"</code>, and every crate compiles under it.</li>
        <li><strong>A small supply chain.</strong> 26 of the 33 library crates have no external runtime dependencies.</li>
        <li><strong>Bare metal.</strong> The causal monad and many other crates build without the standard library.</li>
        <li><strong>Machine-checked laws.</strong> Lean 4 proofs cover the monad laws and more, and a Rust test pins the implementation to each theorem's statement.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where to start</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><a href={`${DOCS_URL}/getting-started/install/`}>Install the library</a> in a Rust project.</li>
        <li>Read the topic that matches your problem, starting with <a href="/overview/dynamic-causality/">dynamic causality</a>.</li>
        <li><a href="/examples/">Browse the worked examples</a> in aerospace, physics, medicine and mathematics.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        DeepCausality is an LF AI &amp; Data Foundation Sandbox project. Read the <a href={GITHUB_URL}>source on GitHub</a> or <a href="/community/">ask the community</a> about your use case.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
